WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.592
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Monday, November second.

2
00:00:04.592 --> 00:00:12.053
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:14.568
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:14.568 --> 00:00:26.864
Today: cyclone evacuations in India, another ship struck in the Strait of Hormuz, hacked emergency alert apps in Russia and Ukraine, an arrest in the Moscow threat case, and an attack in northeastern Kenya.

5
00:00:26.864 --> 00:00:28.260
We start in India.

6
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:40.617
Reuters reports authorities began evacuating people along India's eastern coast today, a day before a severe cyclonic storm is expected to make landfall, in a region still recovering from a deadly cyclone last week.

7
00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:43.225
Reuters says thousands have been evacuated.

8
00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:45.151
Turning to the Strait of Hormuz.

9
00:00:45.151 --> 00:00:49.490
The Institute for the Study of War and the Critical Threats Project report that the U.K.

10
00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:56.060
Maritime Trade Operations center said an unidentified projectile struck a vessel in the Strait of Hormuz yesterday.

11
00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:01.284
They assess Iran likely carried out the strike as part of its efforts to disrupt civilian shipping.

12
00:01:01.284 --> 00:01:03.082
Next, a cyber item.

13
00:01:03.082 --> 00:01:13.929
Radio Free Europe reports hackers targeted emergency alert apps in Russia and Ukraine over the past two days, sending false warnings of nuclear attacks, radiation and chemical threats.

14
00:01:13.929 --> 00:01:21.993
In Ukraine, it says the Air Alert app was compromised and sent fake warnings of radiation, street fighting and an incoming Russian attack.

15
00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:23.949
An update on the Moscow threat.

16
00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:37.083
Russia's state news agency TASS reports the F S B security service said that, while checking U.S. information about a possible attack, it detained a teenager who had posted on TikTok threatening to commit a crime at Tsaritsyno Park.

17
00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:39.076
TRAC first flagged the arrest.

18
00:01:39.076 --> 00:01:40.166
And in Kenya.

19
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:48.966
The Kenyan outlet Kahawa Tungu reports a police officer was shot and injured yesterday in a suspected al Shabaab attack in Bodhai, in Garissa County.

20
00:01:48.966 --> 00:01:51.092
Now a few quick notes by region.

21
00:01:51.092 --> 00:01:53.665
In Europe, an update on southern Ukraine.

22
00:01:53.665 --> 00:02:03.407
Reuters reports Ukraine's military said it evacuated one hundred sixty seven people from threatened areas of the Kherson region as a temporary local ceasefire expired.

23
00:02:03.407 --> 00:02:15.112
Reuters also reports Russian strikes cut power to thousands in Ukraine, and Radio Free Europe reports Russian authorities said more than one hundred drones were destroyed in an attack on the Nizhny Novgorod region.

24
00:02:15.112 --> 00:02:16.123
Separately, the U.S.

25
00:02:16.123 --> 00:02:26.318
State Department says protests over housing and the cost of living are taking place across Portugal, including in Lisbon and Porto, and that police have recently used tear gas.

26
00:02:26.318 --> 00:02:33.951
In the Middle East, Reuters reports, citing health officials, that Israeli strikes killed at least three Palestinians in the Gaza Strip.

27
00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:39.333
And Radio Free Europe reports President Trump again threatened to resume air strikes against Iran.

28
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:49.808
In Africa, Nigerian outlets including Vanguard report Zamfara State police confirmed the rescue of three of six Islamic clerics who were abducted by bandits on September twelfth.

29
00:02:49.808 --> 00:02:57.019
And in cyber, Reuters reports temperatures soared in freezers at Swedish grocery stores, and one store blamed hackers.

30
00:02:57.019 --> 00:02:58.375
That's today's brief.

31
00:02:58.375 --> 00:03:06.568
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

32
00:03:06.568 --> 00:03:14.719
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

33
00:03:14.719 --> 00:03:16.781
This brief is digitally narrated.

34
00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:17.740
Questions?

35
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.232
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

36
00:03:21.232 --> 00:03:23.261
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
